Then you can create instances using Config::default() or use it with other patterns like unwrap_or_default().

Use Functional Options when you need:

1. **Extensibility**: When you want third-party code to provide configuration options without modifying your core struct
2. **Conditional Options**: When options depend on runtime conditions or external factors
3. **Reusable Option Sets**: When you want to compose and reuse sets of options across different instances
4. **API Flexibility**: When you want users to define custom configuration logic

Example scenario in Rust:
```rust
// Different teams can define their own options
mod team_a {
    pub fn with_auth_token(token: String) -> impl FnOnce(super::ConfigBuilder) -> super::ConfigBuilder {
        move |mut config| {
            config.auth_token = Some(token);
            config
        }
    }
}

mod team_b {
    pub fn with_circuit_breaker() -> impl FnOnce(super::ConfigBuilder) -> super::ConfigBuilder {
        |mut config| {
            config.circuit_breaker_enabled = true;
            config
        }
    }
}

// Usage allows mixing options from different sources
let config = ConfigBuilder::new()
    .apply_option(team_a::with_auth_token('secret123'.to_string()))
    .apply_option(team_b::with_circuit_breaker())
    .build();
```

