Use the rstest crate to create parameterized table-driven tests with cleaner syntax:

First, add rstest to your Cargo.toml:
```toml
[dev-dependencies]
rstest = "0.18"
```

Then implement parameterized tests:

```rust
use rstest::rstest;

#[rstest]
#[case("hello", "HELLO")]
#[case("World", "WORLD")]
#[case("Rust", "RUST")]
#[case("", "")]
#[case("123", "123")]
fn test_to_uppercase(#[case] input: &str, #[case] expected: &str) {
    assert_eq!(to_uppercase(input), expected);
}

// For more complex test data:
#[rstest]
#[case(10, 5, 15)]
#[case(-5, 3, -2)]
#[case(0, 0, 0)]
#[case(100, -50, 50)]
fn test_addition(#[case] a: i32, #[case] b: i32, #[case] expected: i32) {
    assert_eq!(add(a, b), expected);
}

// Using table format for better readability:
#[rstest]
#[case::positive_numbers(5, 10, 15)]
#[case::negative_result(-10, 5, -5)]
#[case::zero_values(0, 0, 0)]
fn test_named_cases(#[case] x: i32, #[case] y: i32, #[case] sum: i32) {
    assert_eq!(x + y, sum);
}
```

This approach provides better test names, automatic parameterization, and cleaner failure reporting compared to manual table-driven tests.

